   "strategy": "algebraic_manipulation",
   "sketch": "1. Let x = abs (a + b) and y = abs a + abs b. By the triangle inequality, x ≤ y; also x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0.\n2. Both denominators 1 + x and 1 + y are positive.\n3. It suffices to prove x / (1 + x) ≤ y / (1 + y). Cross-multiplying by the positive denominators gives the equivalent inequality x * (1 + y) ≤ y * (1 + x).\n4. Expanding both sides, this becomes x + x*y ≤ y + x*y, which is equivalent to x ≤ y.\n5. Since x ≤ y holds, the desired inequality follows."
  },
